      "data": {
        "title": "Does a root need a class of its own, or is a custom root just faces and a ground?",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "library-made-current",
          "nodeId": "library-made-current-q-custom-roots"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/classes/nodes/task-planning/open-question.node-class.node",
        "owner": "Michael",
        "status": "open",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1793217661116,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        },
        "body": "Today a root can be drawn through a node class (Custom Declaration), which gives it an editor and its own card faces. Since the vocabulary change the root has Surfaces (chains of faces) and a ground, and AGENTS.md says a root with a custom class keeps its primitive type. If the faces and ground already do the job, the class is a second way to do the same thing and the Declaration Template teaches the wrong one. If it still earns its place (the root's editor, a card), it needs the new names and an example that is correct. My lean: decide after looking at the example together; do not rewrite anything first."
      }
    },
